From 58911c21bf3e1f8a611e8036651065e2e800e04e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 14:26:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rebuild the Helfer view mobile-first from a Claude Design
 handoff
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implemented from design/project/Helferansicht Mobil.dc.html (imported
from the Claude Design project the user shared). Full rewrite of
HelferScreen.tsx, replacing the previous name-filter Picker+TextInput
combo entirely:

- "Wer bist du?" flow: a prominent button opens a search-and-pick panel
  scoped to names actually assigned somewhere in the current event
  (not the whole org-wide helper pool), with a fallback to freely type
  an unlisted name. Once picked, shows a "Du bist X" pill plus a "Nur
  meine Schichten zeigen" toggle that regroups the list by day and
  filters to just that person's shifts.
- Sticky day tabs (weekday + date, two-line) replace the old single
  long scrolling list of all days at once — formatDayTabParts() added
  to schedule.ts for the split label.
- Assigned helpers render as chips, own name pinned first and
  highlighted, collapsed to 4 with a "+N weitere" expand toggle instead
  of wrapping indefinitely (the previous chip redesign still truncated
  awkwardly on shifts with many helpers).
- "Your shift" cards get a light accent tint (colors.accentBg, a
  precomputed ~10% mix since color-mix() has no native iOS/Android
  equivalent), thicker accent left border, and a corner badge;
  unbesetzt shifts get a danger-colored left border instead.
- PDF export demoted to a subtle footer text link — secondary on
  mobile, matching the design intent explicitly discussed beforehand.
- Page is a centered 430px-max column regardless of viewport, mobile
  layout everywhere rather than a separate desktop variant.

App.tsx no longer renders the outer app-chrome Header at all when
accessed via a share link, since HelferScreen now has its own title +
event picker and the old header would just be redundant chrome eating
mobile screen space. Removed the now-dead showAdminTab prop from
Header.tsx that this supersedes.

Hit the same z-index stacking bug fixed earlier for the Admin toolbar
dropdown: the picker panel's own zIndex only matters within its
nearest positioned ancestor's stacking context, so whoBox needed a
zIndex above the sticky day-tabs bar's, not just above its siblings
inside itself.

Verified live at 390px viewport: name search/pick, own-shift
highlighting, chip overflow with 7 assigned helpers, "nur meine"
filtering across days, unbesetzt styling, empty state, and that the
picker panel now correctly renders above the sticky tab bar instead of
behind it.

